[META]
framework: claude-framework
file_id: workflow.REFACTOR
class: universal
version: 1.0.0

[REFACTOR]
overview: Rules specific to refactoring — changing code shape without changing behavior
applies_when: User asks to "refactor / restructure / clean up / reorganize / extract / inline / rename / simplify"
follows: PHASES.toon with extra emphasis on Phase 1 (understand existing behavior) and Phase 5 (prove behavior unchanged)
core_definition: Refactoring changes internal structure. It MUST NOT change observable behavior. If you're changing behavior, it's not a refactor — it's a feature or bugfix.

[RULE_1_BEHAVIOR_PRESERVING]
what: Refactors are behavior-preserving by definition
why: A refactor that also "fixes a bug" or "tightens validation" mixes two concerns; the diff becomes unreviewable
how:
  - In PLAN phase, explicitly state: "No observable behavior changes expected"
  - If you spot a bug during refactor → note it, do NOT fix in the same change
  - If you spot a missing test → add the test FIRST as a separate commit, then refactor on top of it
  - If you think a behavior change is required to refactor → STOP, this is not a refactor, return to PLAN and reclassify

[RULE_2_TESTS_BEFORE_CHANGES]
what: Precondition tests are non-negotiable for refactor
why: Without tests, "behavior-preserving" is aspirational. Tests are how you prove it.
how:
  - In DISCOVER phase: identify existing test coverage for the code being refactored
  - If coverage exists → verify it's green BEFORE any refactor change (baseline)
  - If coverage is thin → add characterization tests FIRST (capture current behavior, even imperfect behavior), get user approval for the added tests, then refactor
  - After refactor → the SAME tests must pass, unchanged

[RULE_3_ONE_TRANSFORMATION_PER_COMMIT]
what: A commit should represent one kind of change (rename, extract function, inline variable, move file)
why: Small, named transformations are reviewable, revertable, and bisectable
how:
  - In PLAN phase, list transformations as an ORDERED sequence of commits, not a single blob
  - Each commit's message names the transformation type
  - If you find yourself writing "refactor X, Y, Z and also..." — split

[RULE_4_EXPLICIT_ROLLBACK_PLAN]
what: Every refactor has an answer to "how do we undo this if it breaks prod?"
why: Refactors often have subtle timing/ordering effects that don't surface until deploy
how:
  - In PLAN phase, include a "Rollback" section: git revert-able? Requires migration reversal? Data compat concerns?
  - For refactors touching hot paths / public APIs / storage layout — answer must be more than "git revert"

[RULE_5_SCOPE_DISCIPLINE]
what: Refactor only what the user asked for; don't opportunistically clean up adjacent code
why: Scope creep in refactors is how 200-line changes become 2000-line changes the user won't merge
how:
  - In PLAN phase, name the exact module/function/file boundary of the refactor
  - "While I'm in here" changes → note as a follow-up task (spawn_task or add to TODO), do NOT include
  - Formatting changes in untouched areas are NOT allowed; keep the diff minimal

[RULE_6_NO_RENAMES_WITHOUT_CONFIRMATION]
what: Renaming symbols, files, or modules is a GATE (see GATES.toon)
why: Renames cascade — downstream callers, docs, configs, logs, search tools all see the change
how:
  - Call out every rename in the plan with old → new
  - Get per-rename approval if multiple renames are bundled
  - Don't rename "for consistency" without the user explicitly asking

[RULE_7_EXTRACTIONS_NEED_EVIDENCE]
what: Extracting a helper / interface / abstraction requires ≥3 concrete call sites OR an explicit architectural requirement
why: Premature extraction is the same problem as premature abstraction in new code — locks in the wrong shape
how:
  - In PLAN phase, list the call sites that will use the extraction
  - If only 1-2 sites → don't extract, inline or leave duplicated

[REFACTOR_PLAN_CHECKLIST]
Before leaving PLAN phase, confirm:
  - [ ] "No observable behavior changes" stated explicitly
  - [ ] Existing test coverage identified and verified green (baseline)
  - [ ] If coverage was insufficient — characterization tests added as a separate preceding step
  - [ ] Transformations listed as an ordered, named sequence
  - [ ] Rollback plan stated
  - [ ] Scope boundary named precisely (file / module / function)
  - [ ] Every rename enumerated with old → new
  - [ ] Every extraction justified with ≥3 call sites or an architectural requirement
